Alejandra: FYI customer reported parser failures after we rolled the invokeEnvelope change. They hit a double-encoded arguments edge and their SDK crashed. Need a short compatibility note + examples for docs.
jamie: can you share the exact failing payload?
Alejandra: pasted from support ticket:
```json
{
  "invokeEnvelope": {
    "tool": "web_search",
    "arguments": "{\"q\": \"redwood inference docs\"}",
    "meta": {"trace_id":"abc123"}
  }
}
```
jamie: aha — arguments is a string (JSON encoded) in that response. Our parsers expect an object. We should warn about "arguments" sometimes being serialized twice and show safe parsing patterns.
marco: I'll add a short TS example showing defensive parsing plus a tiny Go snippet for partners using backend services. Which guide page?
Alejandra: add to /docs/guides/structured-output.md under a new small H3. Title: "Compatibility: tolerant adapters". Keep it short and prescriptive: recommend schema validation then a tiny adapter, not regex hacks.
tasha: CI note: run the link-checker and docs build locally before merging. ci-bot: link-checker failed on PR 4829 earlier, but that was unrelated.
jamie: python snippet (to put in docs):
```py
# normalize_envelope.py
import json

def parse_args(envelope):
    args = envelope.get("arguments")
    if isinstance(args, str):
        # attempt to decode safely
        try:
            return json.loads(args)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            # fall back to empty object to avoid crashes
            return {}
    return args or {}
```
jamie: plus a one-line unit test to show expected output.
marco: TypeScript snippet I'll add:
```ts
// normalizeEnvelope.ts
export function normalizeEnvelope(resp: any) {
  const env = resp.invokeEnvelope ?? resp.tool_call ?? resp;
  let args = env?.arguments;
  if (typeof args === 'string') {
    try {
      args = JSON.parse(args);
    } catch { args = {}; }
  }
  return { ...env, arguments: args };
}
```
marco: Go snippet for backend consumers: small defensive parse using json.RawMessage.
Alejandra: also add a short note: prefer sending arguments as structured objects. Warn against double-encoding in custom tool implementers.
tasha: editorial: avoid using both "compact envelope" and "invoke envelope" interchangeably. Pick one term and define it. Also include a quick regression test in docs repo: sample prompt -> expected normalized JSON.
jamie: I added the unit test example to the PR description to help reviewers.
